  "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) such that the region above the parabola \\(x = y^2 + 1\\) and below the line \\(y = 0\\) is divided into two equal halves by the vertical line \\(x = a\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the area of the region above the parabola and below the line \\(y = 0\\):**\n\n   The parabola \\(x = y^2 + 1\\) intersects the line \\(y = 0\\) at \\(x = 1\\). The area \\(A\\) of the region above the parabola and below the line \\(y = 0\\) can be found by integrating the function \\(x = y^2 + 1\\) from \\(y = -1\\) to \\(y = 1\\):\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\int_{-1}^{1} (y^2 + 1) \\, dy\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating this integral, we get:\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\left[ \\frac{y^3}{3} + y \\right]_{-1}^{1} = \\left( \\frac{1^3}{3} + 1 \\right) - \\left( \\frac{(-1)^3}{3} - 1 \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{3} + 1 \\right) - \\left( -\\frac{1}{3} - 1 \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{3}{3} \\right) - \\left( -\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{3}{3} \\right) = \\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the value of \\(a\\) such that the region is divided into two equal halves:**\n\n   Since the region is divided into two equal halves by the vertical line \\(x = a\\), the area of each half must be \\(\\frac{A}{2} = \\frac{8}{6} = \\frac{4}{3}\\).\n\n   To find the value of \\(a\\), we need to find the point where the area under the line \\(x = a\\) is equal to \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\). This means we need to integrate the function \\(x = a\\) from \\(y = -1\\) to \\(y = 1\\) and set it equal to \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\int_{-1}^{1} a \\, dy = a \\left[ y \\right]_{-1}^{1} = a (1 - (-1)) = a \\cdot 2 = 2a\n   \\]\n\n   Setting this equal to \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\), we get:\n\n   \\[\n   2a = \\frac{4}{3} \\implies a = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\).",
